  "reproduction_readme": "Windows Python 3.14.3; external Python packages, no container, OAuth service, database, GPG or pinentry. Install verification/acceptance-r2/runtime-requirements.txt in a fresh virtual environment. Use the exact frozen product commit as the root. Put that root and verification/acceptance-r2 on PYTHONPATH. Confirm authlib.oauth2.rfc9449.__file__ points at this frozen root; do not install upstream Authlib over it. Keys are generated in memory with cryptography; no private key is saved.\n\nRun python -B -m pytest verification/acceptance-r2/test_review_dpop.py --confcutdir=verification/acceptance-r2 -q --tb=short -p no:cacheprovider -o asyncio_default_fixture_loop_scope=function. Expected frozen behavior: 48 passed, 3 failed (51 total). A correctly signed proof whose ath contains non-ASCII text raises uncaught TypeError. A business replay-store callback that raises an exception containing the synthetic token/proof is rejected, but DEBUG logs and formatted exception cause include those values. This is observed with synthetic closure state, not a claim that the store normally receives raw token/proof in its arguments. Two leak tests share the same exception-detail root cause. There is no demonstrated signature forgery or replay acceptance.\n\npython -B verification/acceptance-r2/review_crypto.py cross-verifies the independent ES256 fixture and RFC7638 thumbprint. python -B verification/acceptance-r2/observe-boundaries.py prints actual TypeError and presence booleans, exits 1 on the frozen defects, and never prints the raw synthetic values. This helper is independently authored after the model turn. Socket connect and create_connection are blocked during the independent suite. The HTTP default-port case alone sets AUTHLIB_INSECURE_TRANSPORT via pytest monkeypatch so the existing OAuth2Request HTTPS policy does not prevent this offline normalization check; the fixture restores the environment afterwards.\n\npython -B -m pytest tests/core -q -p no:cacheprovider reproduces the separately recorded 625 passing core tests. The 43 model DPoP tests are included in those 625; do not add counts. python -B examples/dpop_resource_server.py reproduces the eight model-authored example scenarios (exit 0). The model native trace separately records 852 passed/2 skipped for core+jose and four documentation Python blocks executed; these are not independent full-suite results and overlap other counts. Framework clients, Sphinx documentation build and full prek were not verified because the required tools/dependencies were absent; no all-repository pass is claimed.\n\nacceptance-r1 retains the first independent run (47 pass/4 fail), of which the HTTP InsecureTransportError is an evaluator-fixture issue excluded from scoring. acceptance-r2 fixes only that fixture, without modifying product or previous outputs. The replay-retention fake keeps entries through the inclusive validity endpoint; no separate expiry-cleanup defect is scored. Recording r1 was rejected because a pytest-asyncio warning exposed a local UID path. Recording r2 explicitly sets the fixture loop scope, retains actual failed checks, and continuously captures 75 seconds at 1280x720. Product hashes were unchanged before and after verification. The console is a separate verification tool, not a model-authored UI.\n",
